window.docContents[236].resolve({"/Distance/#section-girth":{"contents":"The girth of a graph is the length of its shortest cycle. Mathlib again provides\ntwo versions: G.egirth : ℕ∞, which is ⊤ for an acyclic graph, and G.girth : ℕ,\nwhich is 0 for an acyclic graph. Girth is the natural companion of the diameter: if\nthe maximum degree is bounded, small diameter forces few vertices, while if the\nminimum degree is bounded below, large girth forces many. These are the Moore and\ncage bounds of Moore And Cage Bounds. Girth also enters the\nMycielski construction: mμ[G] raises the chromatic number\n(Colouring Mycielskians) while staying triangle-free\n(Triangle Free Mycielskians), and below we compute its girth\nexactly.\n\nA graph with its shortest cycle highlighted\n\n#check egirth  -- G.egirth : ℕ∞\n#check girth   -- G.girth : ℕ\n\n\nThe girth is infinite (respectively zero) iff the graph is acyclic, and any cycle\nbounds it above. Every cycle has length at least 3, so egirth is always at least\n3, but girth is at least 3 only for a graph with a cycle, because of the junk\nvalue 0.\n\n#check egirth_eq_top                  -- G.egirth = ⊤ ↔ G.IsAcyclic\n#check girth_eq_zero                  -- G.girth = 0 ↔ G.IsAcyclic\n#check Walk.IsCycle.egirth_le_length  -- p.IsCycle → G.egirth ≤ p.length\n#check three_le_egirth                -- 3 ≤ G.egirth\n#check three_le_girth                 -- ¬G.IsAcyclic → 3 ≤ G.girth\n\n\nGirth and diameter are related: a shortest cycle has length at most 2 * diam + 1,\nsince otherwise two antipodal vertices of the cycle would be joined by a shorter\npath, producing a shorter cycle. Mathlib states this in three ways, and their\nhypotheses show the junk values at work.\n\n* For egirth we must exclude acyclic graphs, whose egirth is ⊤.* For girth no hypothesis is needed: the junk value 0 of an acyclic graph\n  satisfies the bound anyway.* To use diam instead of ediam we must assume that the diameter is finite, since\n  otherwise diam is 0 and the right-hand side is just 1.\n\nSo a junk value sometimes removes a hypothesis and sometimes adds one. The same\nhappens for containment: a graph containing a copy of H has girth at most that of\nH, and the girth version needs H to have a cycle. Finally, isomorphisms\npreserve the girth.\n\n#check egirth_le_two_mul_ediam_add_one -- ¬G.IsAcyclic → G.egirth ≤ 2 * G.ediam + 1\n#check girth_le_two_mul_ediam_add_one  -- G.girth ≤ 2 * G.ediam + 1\n#check girth_le_two_mul_diam_add_one   -- G.ediam ≠ ⊤ → G.girth ≤ 2 * G.diam + 1\n#check IsContained.egirth_le           -- H ⊑ G → G.egirth ≤ H.egirth\n#check IsContained.girth_le            -- H ⊑ G → ¬H.IsAcyclic → G.girth ≤ H.girth\n#check Iso.egirth_eq\n\n\nA graph has no cycle of length at most k exactly when it contains no copy of\nC[n] for 3 ≤ n ≤ k, i.e. when it is C[n]-free in the language of\nCopies: Mathlib's cycleGraph_isContained_iff says that G\ncontains a copy of C[n] (for n ≥ 3) iff it has a cycle of length n. (Every\ncycle has length at least 3, so only n ≥ 3 matters; C[2] is a single edge.)\nExcluding these copies therefore bounds the girth from below.\n\nlemma le_egirth_of_free {k : ℕ} (h : ∀ n, 3 ≤ n → n ≤ k → C[n].Free G) :\n    k + 1 ≤ G.egirth := by\n  rw [le_egirth]\n  intro a w hw\n  by_contra! hlt\n  have hk : w.length ≤ k := by exact_mod_cast Order.le_of_lt_add_one hlt\n  have h3 := hw.three_le_length\n  exact h _ h3 hk ((cycleGraph_isContained_iff (by grind)).mpr ⟨_, _, hw, rfl⟩)\n\n\nBeing triangle-free is the case n = 3: C[3] is the complete graph on three\nvertices (cycleGraph_three_eq_top), and Mathlib's cliqueFree_iff_free_top_fin\nidentifies G.CliqueFree n with being K[n]-free.\n\nlemma cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph : G.CliqueFree 3 ↔ C[3].Free G := by\n  rw [cycleGraph_three_eq_top, cliqueFree_iff_free_top_fin]\n\n\nSo a triangle-free graph has girth at least 4, and if it also contains no copy of\nC[4] then its girth is at least 5.\n\nlemma four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three (h : G.CliqueFree 3) : 4 ≤ G.egirth :=\n  le_egirth_of_free fun n h3 hn => by\n    obtain rfl : n = 3 := by grind\n    exact cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph.mp h\n\n\nlemma five_le_egirth (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3) (h4 : C[4].Free G) : 5 ≤ G.egirth :=\n  le_egirth_of_free fun n h3n hn => by\n    obtain rfl | rfl : n = 3 ∨ n = 4 := by grind\n    exacts [cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph.mp h3, h4]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance","header":"11.3. Girth","id":"/Distance/#section-girth"},"/Introduction/#section-how-to-read-this-guide":{"contents":"If you are reading the .lean files directly then see the README.md file for build\ninstructions (lake exe cache get followed by lake build). Each chapter is in a\nseparate folder that also contains any associated exercises.\n\nIf you are reading the web version then please note that this is published using\nVerso. You should be able to see most of the\ninteractive features of Lean such as InfoView messages and #command outputs.\n\nTo see the actual output of a #command you need to hover over the #command itself\nwith your cursor (the #command will have wavy underlining). Many terms also have\nassociated doc-strings which you can see by hovering over them; click on them to view\nthe full documentation. Types of terms are also shown.\n\nFor example, below you should be able to hover over the #source command to see the\nverbatim source-code of the definition of SimpleGraph in Mathlib. While if you\nhover over SimpleGraph itself you will see its doc-string; clicking on it will take\nyou to its documentation page. Hovering over the #check will tell you the type of\nG.Adj u v (it is a Prop); while hovering over u will confirm that it has type\nV; which in turn you can hover over to see its type Type u_1.\n\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} (u v : V)\n#source SimpleGraph\n#check G.Adj u v -- Prop\n#check sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges -- ∑ v, G.degree v = 2 * G.edgeFinset.card\n\n\nThroughout this guide we work in the namespace SimpleGraph, so for example Adj\nmeans SimpleGraph.Adj, and our definitions get names like SimpleGraph.pathNat₁.\nWhen we want to put results or definitions in other namespaces we will do this\nexplicitly.\n\nIn the interest of making this guide as readable as possible by graph theorists we\nintroduce quite a lot of \"standard\" graph theory notation that is not in Mathlib,\n(for example K[n], C[n], K[s, t], Q[n], ω G, χ G, etc.) For reference see\nNotation. We also provide lists of the\nDefinitions introduced in the guide and of the main\nResults proved in it.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.2. How to read this guide","id":"/Introduction/#section-how-to-read-this-guide"}});